  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Dry with below normal temperatures through\nTuesday night.\n\nA large high pressure arrives from the west tonight. Gusty winds\nsubside around sunset, then become light and variable late this\nevening. Expecting decent radiational cooling conditions,\nleading to low temperatures about 5-10 degrees below normal. The\ncooler conditions continue into Tuesday night with light E to SE\nwinds. This high pressure should be over the North Atlantic\nTuesday night, shifting winds more from the S to SW. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Above normal temperatures return midweek and \nlast through the end of the week. Dry weather dominates, though \na few showers are possible sometime in the late Thursday-early \nFriday timeframe.\n\nHigh pressure continues to dominate this week's pattern, keeping \ngenerally dry conditions in place for southern New England. A strong \nupper level ridge set up across the southeast US will build partly \ninto the northeastern US midweek before flattening out on its \nnorthern tier Thursday into Friday. A trough remains well to our \nnorth in Canada, and between these two large features, surface high \npressure remains affixed over much of the northern and northeastern \nUS. With a surge in warmer mid level air coming in from the west \nduring this period, more above-normal temperatures make a return \nacross southern New England; highs climb back up into the 70s and \nmay creep into the low 80s.\n\nHigh pressure loosens its grip over the region slightly late \nThursday into Friday as a shortwave tracks toward the region. NAEFS \nguidance signaled a return of more anomalous moisture with the \nwesterly flow, indicating PWATs about a standard deviation or two \nabove normal for this time of year. Ahead of any possible showers \nwith the incoming shortwave/frontal system, it may feel a bit more \nhumid on Thursday. Guidance currently favors showers moving through \nthe region late Thursday into Friday morning, though timing may \nfluctuate slightly between now and then. Dry conditions follow as \ndrier air aloft is advected in, though the higher than normal \ntemperatures may remain Friday afternoon before cooler temperatures \ntake hold for Saturday.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...Possible return for unsettled weather towards\nthe back half of the upcoming weekend.\n\nThe ridge to our south appears to break down a bit going into the \nweekend, which may introduce increased chances for more unsettled \nconditions. Latest ensemble guidance hints at the second half of the \nweekend as the likely timeframe with strong low pressure tracking \ntowards the region from the Plains and Great Lakes. Given how\nfar out this event is, much will likely change over future\nupdates.",
